./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:271:## Claim: Raw \(\beta\) may fail because of rare histories while average truncation succeeds
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:275:Evidence: Essential sup can be driven by histories of tiny unconditional probability, while \(\Delta_T(H)/T\) averages over realized histories.
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:279:## Claim: Raw \(\beta\) can fail because long delayed hits have tiny value
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:282:Dependencies: Infinite deterministic exogenous process; raw infinite-process \(\beta(h)=\sup_t\sup_a\Pr(h<\tau_t(a)<\infty\mid P_{1:t})\); regret against \(\sup_a\sum_{t\le T}r_t(a)\).
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:283:Evidence: Let block \(m\) consist of \(L_m=2^m\) zeros followed by a spike \(p_m=2^{-2m}\), and set \(S_m=\sum_{j\le m}(L_j+1)\). For any \(h\), choose \(m\) with \(L_m>h\), take \(t=S_{m-1}+1\), and quote \(a=p_m/2\). Deterministically \(\tau_t(a)=L_m>h\), so \(\beta(h)=1\). For any horizon \(T\) and quote \(a\), let \(M=\max\{m:S_m\le T,\ p_m>a\}\), if nonempty. Since \(p_m\) decreases strictly, only times \(t<S_M\) can trade, and
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:288:If no such \(M\) exists, the reward is zero. Thus \(\sup_T\sup_a\sum_{t\le T}r_t(a)\le1/2\), and the zero-quote learner has \(O(1)\) regret despite \(\beta(h)\equiv1\).
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:289:Known gaps: This is an infinite-process/raw-\(\beta\) obstruction. For finite-horizon \(\beta_T\), tails are trivially zero once \(h\ge T\); the right finite-horizon reading is that no uniform raw-delay tail is necessary. The optional claim \(\sup_T\Delta_T(H)=O(1/H)\) is not yet proved.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:3:## Original "only if beta" conjecture
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:4:Attempt: Prove that sublinear regret is possible only if the essential-sup hitting tail \(\beta(h)\to0\).
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:5:Failure: False under law-aware/process-wise quantifiers. For iid uniform prices, \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\), but a learner knowing the law can play the best fixed quote in expectation and get zero regret. Iteration 2 strengthened this: for every finite-horizon known exogenous law, a horizon-aware conditional grid oracle gets regret at most \(T/K\).
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:11:Failure: The conditional grid oracle gives \(R_T\le T/K\) for every such law. With \(K=T\), regret is \(O(1)\) independent of \(\beta\), \(\Delta\), or \(\delta\).
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:15:## Treating \(\Delta_T(H)=o(T)\) as a characterization
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:16:Attempt: Replace \(\beta\) by the average/worst-action truncation loss \(\Delta_T(H)\) and claim necessity.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:17:Failure: Singleton terminal-spike law has zero law-aware regret but \(\Delta_T(H)=\Omega(T)\) for \(H=o(T)\).
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:89:Lesson: Use this result as a precise finite-class value characterization and a diagnostic for lower-bound construction. Keep the separate \(\delta_T(H)\) upper theorem as the tail-based sufficient condition.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:100:Failure: For fixed \(D\), delay is bounded by \(D\), so asymptotic regret is \(O(D)=o(T)\) and \(\beta(h)=0\) for \(h\ge D\).
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:117:Attempt: Salvage raw \(\beta(h)\to0\) necessity by looking for deterministic long-delay examples with sublinear regret.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:118:Failure: The tiny-price block construction has \(L_m=2^m\) zeros followed by \(p_m=2^{-2m}\), giving \(\beta(h)=1\) for all finite \(h\), but \(\sup_T\sup_a\sum_{t\le T}r_t(a)\le1/2\). The zero-quote learner therefore has \(O(1)\) regret.
./iterations/iter_004/consolidation.md:3:- Kept prior accepted results intact: known-law oracle, \(\delta_T(H)\) upper bound, posterior Bayes-gap lower certificate, and hidden-terminal/\(q\)-scaled atoms.
./working_notes/promising_directions.md:13:## 3. Write the clean sufficient theorem with \(\delta_T(H)\)
