./iterations/iter_006/idea_02/explorer_01.md:42:**Lemma 1:** The deterministic tiny-price process has \(\beta(h)=1\) for every finite \(h\).
./iterations/iter_006/idea_02/explorer_01.md:51:A useful extra observation: this example is not an obstruction to value-weighted truncation criteria. One can likely show \(\sup_T \Delta_T(H)=O(1/H)\), because long delays occur only at spike values \(p_m\asymp L_m^{-2}\).
./iterations/iter_006/idea_02/explorer_01.md:63:The counterexample targets the infinite-process \(\beta(h)=\sup_t\sup_a\cdots\) definition. If someone instead defines a fixed finite-horizon \(\beta_T(h)\), then \(\beta_T(h)=0\) trivially for \(h\ge T\); the right finite-horizon statement would be that no uniform-in-\(T\) raw-delay tail is necessary.
./iterations/iter_006/idea_02/explorer_01.md:67:This does not refute \(\delta_T(H)\)-style sufficient conditions; it supports the view that value-weighted delayed reward is the right object.
./iterations/iter_006/idea_02/explorer_01.md:71:This is a deterministic, no-estimation, singleton-law obstruction to any theorem saying raw \(\beta(h)\to0\) is necessary for sublinear regret. The obstruction is specifically that raw hitting delay ignores payoff scale.
