./iterations/iter_003/promising_ideas.json:1:{"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The resulting object may be close to the full minimax value and therefore hard to compute. It may also fail to yield clean scalar tail conditions unless extra closure or richness assumptions are imposed on the class.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","information-complexity lower bound","model taxonomy"],"summary":"Generalize the hidden-terminal construction into a Bayes-gap delay modulus: for a class C_T, find pairs or mixtures of exogenous laws whose observable prefixes, or H-truncated reward feedback, are indistinguishable for many rounds but whose best fixed quote reward curves are separated. Prove a Yao/Le Cam lower bound in terms of the accumulated Bayes gap.","title":"Bayes-Gap Delay Modulus","why_promising":"This targets the real obstruction identified by the solver attempt: valuable delayed reward plus learner ignorance, not raw hitting delay. It could become the necessary counterpart to the delta_T(H) upper theorem."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Naive repetition can erase the gap because the comparator is a single fixed quote in expectation. The construction must avoid the iid block-splicing failure already recorded in the notes.","suggested_lenses":["finite-horizon lower bound","rate matching","counterexample search"],"summary":"Complete the q-scaled hidden-terminal family. Define the no-reveal event exactly, compute beta, Delta, delta, and the Bayes gap, then test whether horizon-indexed classes with prescribed tail profile can force regret of order qD or produce power-tail lower rates matching the truncation upper bound.","title":"q-Scaled Tail Lower Bounds","why_promising":"It is the most concrete route from the known finite-horizon lower-bound atom to quantitative minimax rates in terms of valuable delayed tail mass."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"A clever anytime strategy may trade off the two deadlines better than the current sketch suggests. Earlier and later blocks must be made negligible by super-growing lengths without accidentally changing the fixed comparator benchmark.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","horizon-free quantifiers","formal proof"],"summary":"Formalize the candidate deterministic known-law anytime obstruction using super-growing blocks with two deadlines, for example many zeros, then price 1/2, then many zeros, then price near 1. Show that any single horizon-free policy suffers large regret at one of the two deadline horizons, despite knowing the entire infinite sequence.","title":"Anytime Known-Law Obstruction","why_promising":"The oracle lemma is horizon-aware. This direction could identify a genuinely different characterization for anytime policies, where tail behavior may matter again even for known exogenous processes."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"If the bad histories have too little probability, the example may only refute beta while being mathematically uninteresting. If they have too much probability, delta or regret may become linear.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","tail calculation","average-vs-essential-sup comparison"],"summary":"Build an explicit stochastic process where rare histories lead to a state from which a long delayed hit is certain, keeping the essential-sup beta(h) large, while the unconditional contribution to Delta_T(H), delta_T(H), and expected regret remains o(T).","title":"Rare-History Beta Failure","why_promising":"It would cleanly separate essential-sup raw tails from average valuable delayed reward, strengthening the case that beta is too strong even as a sufficient proxy."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The fixed comparator might exploit many tiny terminal prices by choosing an even tinier quote, so the block lengths and prices need careful asymptotic balancing.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","asymptotic estimates","reward-magnitude audit"],"summary":"Construct deterministic or stochastic long zero blocks ending in prices p_m decreasing to 0, with block lengths increasing. Aim for beta(h)=1 along arbitrarily long delays, but total best fixed comparator value, or delayed valuable tail, sublinear along all horizons.","title":"Low-Value Long Delays","why_promising":"This isolates a second flaw in raw hitting tails: they ignore reward magnitude. A successful construction would be a simple refutation of any necessity statement based only on delay probability."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Standard delayed-experts theorems may not apply because non-hits can remain censored until the horizon, and quote-specific feedback arrives at different times. The final condition may be less clean than the H-truncation theorem.","suggested_lenses":["upper-bound proof","algorithm design","delayed-feedback literature"],"summary":"Replace hard truncation by adaptive delayed-experts bounds depending on total outstanding effective delay or outstanding value of grid rewards. Try to prove bounds of the form sqrt((T + D_eff) log K) plus discretization, where D_eff discounts low-value or predictable pending rewards.","title":"Outstanding-Value Upper Bounds","why_promising":"This may improve the sufficient condition beyond existence of a single H with small delta_T(H), and it may suggest the right quantity to match in lower bounds."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The framework may become too abstract or tautological unless explorers identify computable complexity measures for natural classes. It also requires careful distinction between known-law conditional expectations and estimable unknown-law structure.","suggested_lenses":["statistical learning theory","sequential covering","predictability characterization"],"summary":"View the known-law oracle as playing the conditional revenue curve g_t^P(a)=E_P[a 1{max future price exceeds a} | history]. For unknown exogenous classes, study online learnability of the family of such conditional reward functions under public histories, using sequential covers, eluder dimension, or prediction-with-expert-advice complexity.","title":"Conditional Revenue Learnability","why_promising":"It directly captures why known laws are easy and hidden-terminal classes are hard: the issue is predictability of reward curves, not the delay tail alone."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"This may be outside the intended model if prices are meant to be exogenous. The explorer must avoid mixing policy regret, realized-path regret, and counterfactual comparator regret.","suggested_lenses":["model audit","counterexample search","formal definitions"],"summary":"Audit the action-dependent case separately. Define possible causal semantics for P_t depending on past quotes, then build minimal examples showing that the exogenous delayed-Hedge reduction fails or that linear regret is unavoidable under some natural comparator definitions.","title":"Action-Dependent Protocol Split","why_promising":"The upper theorem is only sound for public exogenous prices. A precise split prevents a false theorem if the original market-making protocol allows adaptive market responses."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
