./logs/iter_001_ideas.stderr.log:1054:{"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"This may only repackage the upper bound and not give necessity. It may also require horizon-aware choices of H and K or prior knowledge of the tail profile unless a doubling/adaptive scheme is added.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","quantifier audit","rate optimization"],"summary":"Turn the solver's Delta_T(H) truncation into a precise theorem for a process class C: for any H,K there is a grid delayed-full-information learner with sup_{P in C} R_T(P) <= C sqrt(T H log K)+T/K+sup_{P in C} Delta_T^P(H), where Delta_T^P(H)=sup_a sum_t E[r_t(a)-r_t^H(a)]. Then derive exact qualitative condition: existence of H_T,K_T with H_T log K_T=o(T), T/K_T=o(T), and uniform Delta_T(H_T)=o(T).","title":"Uniform Delta Tail Upper Bound","why_promising":"It is the cleanest rigorous sufficient condition already latent in the solver attempt, strictly weaker than the beta essential-sup condition, and gives immediate rate formulas once Delta_T is estimated."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"This will prove a minimax obstruction for broad unknown classes, not a process-wise necessity theorem. Care is needed if the problem forbids horizon-dependent processes or assumes a fixed stochastic law.","suggested_lenses":["lower bound","Yao principle","counterexample search"],"summary":"Formalize a finite-horizon indistinguishability lower bound. Use two processes with identical prefix P_1=...=P_D=0 and different terminal reveal P_{D+1}=p0 or p1, e.g. p0=1/2 and p1=1. Before the reveal, any learner must use the same quote distribution under both worlds, but the best fixed quote is near p0 in one world and near p1 in the other, yielding constant regret per early round and Omega(D) total regret. Then explore block-splicing to obtain a fixed infinite process or class-level lower bound.","title":"Hidden Terminal Price Lower Bound","why_promising":"It directly captures why long unobserved hitting delays can force linear regret in distribution-free settings and gives a crisp target for critics to verify."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The right sigma-field and norm may become too abstract to yield a usable theorem. Known-law predictability can make every process trivial, so the condition must be tied to what the learner is allowed to know or estimate.","suggested_lenses":["characterization","martingale decomposition","statistical decision theory"],"summary":"Replace raw long-delay mass by unpredictable long-delay value. Decompose z_t^H(a)=r_t(a)-r_t^H(a) into a predictable component, such as E[z_t^H(a)|available short-horizon information], and an innovation/residual component. Seek an upper bound depending on learnable predictable surrogates plus the residual tail, and a lower bound from pairs of laws with small observable-distance but separated predictable tail values.","title":"Predictability-Aware Tail Condition","why_promising":"It explains both sides of the solver attempt: iid-uniform has large raw beta but predictable expected rewards, while hidden-block examples have genuinely unpredictable delayed value."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"This addresses stochastic i.i.d. or mixing subclasses rather than adversarial adaptive processes. Endpoint behavior and atoms can make the optimal quote move with T and complicate uniform convergence.","suggested_lenses":["positive example","empirical process","rate derivation"],"summary":"Study the unknown i.i.d. price class as a concrete countermodel to beta-necessity. Since prices are observed immediately, estimate F from P_1,...,P_t and optimize G_T(a)=sum_{L=0}^{T-1} a(1-F(a)^L). Prove sublinear regret from DKW/threshold uniform convergence, including cases like continuous support up to 1 where beta(h)=1 for all h.","title":"Unknown IID Prices Despite Beta Failure","why_promising":"It could produce a strong theorem showing that even law-unaware learners can beat the beta condition in natural stochastic classes, not just in the solver's law-aware example."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"Near-optimal sets are horizon-dependent and may be unknown to the learner. If bad long-tail actions are needed for exploration, a near-optimal-only condition may be insufficient.","suggested_lenses":["refined sufficient condition","localization","counterexample search"],"summary":"Investigate localized tail conditions over relevant actions rather than all a. Define a near-optimal set A_T(epsilon)={a: V_T(a) >= sup_b V_T(b)-epsilon T} and a localized truncation Delta_T^{loc}(H,epsilon)=sup_{a in A_T(epsilon)} sum_t E[r_t(a)-r_t^H(a)]. Try to combine localization/experts-with-elimination with truncation so the algorithm pays long-delay cost only for plausible comparator actions.","title":"Localized Tail Around Good Quotes","why_promising":"The beta supremum is often driven by pathological quotes near the top of the support. Regret should only care about actions that can compete with the best fixed quote."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"General delayed-experts lower bounds may not embed into the market-making threshold reward structure. The threshold class may have lower complexity and better rates than arbitrary K experts.","suggested_lenses":["rate lower bound","embedding","sequential complexity"],"summary":"Seek matching rates for the upper bound. For bounded delay H, prove or refute an Omega(sqrt(T H)) lower bound within the market-making model, not just generic delayed experts. Try to encode hard threshold decisions through carefully chosen future price maxima M_{t,H}=max_{s<=H} P_{t+s}, so r_t^H(a)=a 1{a<M_{t,H}} realizes a rich enough online threshold class.","title":"Matching Delay-Rate Lower Bounds","why_promising":"The current upper rate sqrt(T H log K)+T/K may be loose because market-making rewards are one-dimensional thresholds. Matching or improving it is needed for a full rate theorem."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"This is foundational rather than a final characterization. It may conclude that the original question is ill-posed unless the model class and learner knowledge are fixed.","suggested_lenses":["definition audit","theorem statement","obstruction"],"summary":"Write a quantifier taxonomy. Prove that for a known process law, regret to the best fixed quote is trivial: play an exact or epsilon-optimal fixed quote for the horizon, giving zero or O(1) regret. Separate this from universal guarantees over a class, minimax guarantees, individual-sequence regret, horizon-dependent adversaries, and adaptive stochastic processes.","title":"Quantifier Taxonomy And Triviality","why_promising":"A necessary-and-sufficient condition cannot be correct until 'possible' is pinned down. This direction prevents proving a false necessity statement and guides all lower-bound claims."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"Algorithmic exactness may distract from the statistical characterization. Continuous-action algorithms may still need discretization near atoms or strict inequalities.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","threshold geometry","upper-bound improvement"],"summary":"Exploit the continuum threshold structure directly. For truncated horizon H, after H steps the learner observes r_t^H(a)=a 1{a<M_{t,H}} for all a, a piecewise-linear/threshold reward curve. Investigate whether exponential weights or FTRL can be implemented over intervals without a K-grid and whether regret can be bounded by threshold-class complexity rather than log K plus T/K discretization.","title":"Continuum Threshold Learner","why_promising":"It may sharpen upper rates and remove discretization artifacts, making any eventual characterization cleaner and less tied to arbitrary grids."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stdout.log:5:The main weakness is not the calculation; it is the quantifier scope. The construction does **not** prove a process-wise necessity theorem for the raw tail condition \(\beta(h)\to0\). It only shows that if the learner must work uniformly over a class containing two indistinguishable horizon-dependent processes, then long hidden delays can force \(\Omega(D)\) regret at horizon \(T=D+1\).
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stdout.log:16:   The clean construction chooses \(D=T-1\). If one fixes \(D\) and extends the process to an infinite sequence, the delay tail becomes bounded and \(\beta(h)=0\) for \(h\ge D\). So the example gives a one-horizon obstruction, not an asymptotic impossibility for a fixed process.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stdout.log:19:   The \(qD/8\) claim is likely correct, but it needs a formal law: what happens on “no reveal,” how the process is extended beyond the finite horizon, and for which histories/actions \(\beta(h)\ge q\). As stated, it supports the heuristic \(T\beta(H)\), but is not yet a theorem matching the upper-bound term.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stdout.log:41:   Let \(P_1=\cdots=P_D=0\), \(P_{D+1}=Y\), and then \(P_t=0\) forever. The finite-horizon regret can be \(\Omega(D)\), but asymptotically regret is \(O(D)=o(T)\), and \(\beta(h)=0\) for \(h\ge D\). So the construction does not refute sublinear asymptotic regret for that fixed process.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stdout.log:43:3. **Tiny-price long-delay obstruction to raw \(\beta\).**  
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stdout.log:44:   Consider deterministic blocks of many zeros followed by prices \(p_m\downarrow0\), with block lengths going to infinity. For every \(h\), some block has delay \(>h\), so \(\beta(h)=1\). But the best fixed quote’s total reward can be \(o(T)\) if the block lengths grow fast enough and \(p_m\to0\). Always quoting \(0\) then has sublinear regret. This strongly suggests raw \(\beta(h)\to0\) is not necessary.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stdout.log:67:The explorer’s caution that raw \(\beta\) is probably too strong as a necessity condition is also well supported.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stdout.log:73:2. Promote the tiny-price long-delay example into a rigorous counterexample to necessity of raw \(\beta(h)\to0\).
./logs/iter_003_ideas.stdout.log:1:{"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The resulting object may be close to the full minimax value and therefore hard to compute. It may also fail to yield clean scalar tail conditions unless extra closure or richness assumptions are imposed on the class.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","information-complexity lower bound","model taxonomy"],"summary":"Generalize the hidden-terminal construction into a Bayes-gap delay modulus: for a class C_T, find pairs or mixtures of exogenous laws whose observable prefixes, or H-truncated reward feedback, are indistinguishable for many rounds but whose best fixed quote reward curves are separated. Prove a Yao/Le Cam lower bound in terms of the accumulated Bayes gap.","title":"Bayes-Gap Delay Modulus","why_promising":"This targets the real obstruction identified by the solver attempt: valuable delayed reward plus learner ignorance, not raw hitting delay. It could become the necessary counterpart to the delta_T(H) upper theorem."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Naive repetition can erase the gap because the comparator is a single fixed quote in expectation. The construction must avoid the iid block-splicing failure already recorded in the notes.","suggested_lenses":["finite-horizon lower bound","rate matching","counterexample search"],"summary":"Complete the q-scaled hidden-terminal family. Define the no-reveal event exactly, compute beta, Delta, delta, and the Bayes gap, then test whether horizon-indexed classes with prescribed tail profile can force regret of order qD or produce power-tail lower rates matching the truncation upper bound.","title":"q-Scaled Tail Lower Bounds","why_promising":"It is the most concrete route from the known finite-horizon lower-bound atom to quantitative minimax rates in terms of valuable delayed tail mass."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"A clever anytime strategy may trade off the two deadlines better than the current sketch suggests. Earlier and later blocks must be made negligible by super-growing lengths without accidentally changing the fixed comparator benchmark.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","horizon-free quantifiers","formal proof"],"summary":"Formalize the candidate deterministic known-law anytime obstruction using super-growing blocks with two deadlines, for example many zeros, then price 1/2, then many zeros, then price near 1. Show that any single horizon-free policy suffers large regret at one of the two deadline horizons, despite knowing the entire infinite sequence.","title":"Anytime Known-Law Obstruction","why_promising":"The oracle lemma is horizon-aware. This direction could identify a genuinely different characterization for anytime policies, where tail behavior may matter again even for known exogenous processes."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"If the bad histories have too little probability, the example may only refute beta while being mathematically uninteresting. If they have too much probability, delta or regret may become linear.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","tail calculation","average-vs-essential-sup comparison"],"summary":"Build an explicit stochastic process where rare histories lead to a state from which a long delayed hit is certain, keeping the essential-sup beta(h) large, while the unconditional contribution to Delta_T(H), delta_T(H), and expected regret remains o(T).","title":"Rare-History Beta Failure","why_promising":"It would cleanly separate essential-sup raw tails from average valuable delayed reward, strengthening the case that beta is too strong even as a sufficient proxy."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The fixed comparator might exploit many tiny terminal prices by choosing an even tinier quote, so the block lengths and prices need careful asymptotic balancing.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","asymptotic estimates","reward-magnitude audit"],"summary":"Construct deterministic or stochastic long zero blocks ending in prices p_m decreasing to 0, with block lengths increasing. Aim for beta(h)=1 along arbitrarily long delays, but total best fixed comparator value, or delayed valuable tail, sublinear along all horizons.","title":"Low-Value Long Delays","why_promising":"This isolates a second flaw in raw hitting tails: they ignore reward magnitude. A successful construction would be a simple refutation of any necessity statement based only on delay probability."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Standard delayed-experts theorems may not apply because non-hits can remain censored until the horizon, and quote-specific feedback arrives at different times. The final condition may be less clean than the H-truncation theorem.","suggested_lenses":["upper-bound proof","algorithm design","delayed-feedback literature"],"summary":"Replace hard truncation by adaptive delayed-experts bounds depending on total outstanding effective delay or outstanding value of grid rewards. Try to prove bounds of the form sqrt((T + D_eff) log K) plus discretization, where D_eff discounts low-value or predictable pending rewards.","title":"Outstanding-Value Upper Bounds","why_promising":"This may improve the sufficient condition beyond existence of a single H with small delta_T(H), and it may suggest the right quantity to match in lower bounds."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The framework may become too abstract or tautological unless explorers identify computable complexity measures for natural classes. It also requires careful distinction between known-law conditional expectations and estimable unknown-law structure.","suggested_lenses":["statistical learning theory","sequential covering","predictability characterization"],"summary":"View the known-law oracle as playing the conditional revenue curve g_t^P(a)=E_P[a 1{max future price exceeds a} | history]. For unknown exogenous classes, study online learnability of the family of such conditional reward functions under public histories, using sequential covers, eluder dimension, or prediction-with-expert-advice complexity.","title":"Conditional Revenue Learnability","why_promising":"It directly captures why known laws are easy and hidden-terminal classes are hard: the issue is predictability of reward curves, not the delay tail alone."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"This may be outside the intended model if prices are meant to be exogenous. The explorer must avoid mixing policy regret, realized-path regret, and counterfactual comparator regret.","suggested_lenses":["model audit","counterexample search","formal definitions"],"summary":"Audit the action-dependent case separately. Define possible causal semantics for P_t depending on past quotes, then build minimal examples showing that the exogenous delayed-Hedge reduction fails or that linear regret is unavoidable under some natural comparator definitions.","title":"Action-Dependent Protocol Split","why_promising":"The upper theorem is only sound for public exogenous prices. A precise split prevents a false theorem if the original market-making protocol allows adaptive market responses."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
