./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1524:./logs/iter_002_solver.stderr.log:583:Attempt: Prove that sublinear regret is possible only if the essential-sup hitting tail \(\beta(h)\to0\).
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1525:./logs/iter_002_solver.stderr.log:584:Failure: False under law-aware/process-wise quantifiers. For iid uniform prices, \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\), but a learner knowing the law can play the best fixed quote in expectation and get zero regret.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1526:./logs/iter_002_solver.stderr.log:590:Failure: Singleton terminal-spike law has zero law-aware regret but \(\Delta_T(H)=\Omega(T)\) for \(H=o(T)\).
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1529:./logs/iter_002_solver.stderr.log:608:Failure: For fixed \(D\), delay is bounded by \(D\), so asymptotic regret is \(O(D)=o(T)\) and \(\beta(h)=0\) for \(h\ge D\).
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1533:./logs/iter_002_solver.stderr.log:627:Thus \(\beta(h)=\sup_{a<1}a^h=1\) for every \(h\).
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1534:./logs/iter_002_solver.stderr.log:628:Why it matters: If the law is known, playing a maximizer of \(G_T(a)=\sum_{L=0}^{T-1}a(1-a^L)\) every round matches the fixed comparator in expectation, giving zero regret despite failed \(\beta\).
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1537:./logs/iter_002_solver.stderr.log:634:Facts: For each early quote, expected reward is \(a(1-a)\), maximized at \(a=1/2\). The learner can quote \(1/2\) and get zero regret. For any \(H=o(T)\), most early rewards are excluded by truncation, so \(\Delta_T(H)\approx (T-H)/4=\Omega(T)\).
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1542:./logs/iter_002_solver.stderr.log:650:## Rare bad histories can break essential sup without large average loss
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1543:./logs/iter_002_solver.stderr.log:652:Facts needed: Essential supremum over histories can keep \(\beta(H)\) large, while \(\Delta_T(H)/T\) remains small because the histories almost never occur.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1544:./logs/iter_002_solver.stderr.log:653:Why it matters: Would show strict weakness of average truncation criteria compared with \(\beta\).
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1545:./logs/iter_002_solver.stderr.log:657:## Tiny-price long-delay blocks may break raw beta without meaningful regret
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1546:./logs/iter_002_solver.stderr.log:659:Facts needed: For every \(h\), some block creates a conditional state with \(\Pr(h<\tau<\infty)=1\), so \(\beta(h)=1\). But if \(p_m\) decreases fast enough, total best fixed comparator value may be \(o(T)\), making sublinear regret trivial.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1548:./logs/iter_002_solver.stderr.log:681:\delta_T^P(H)=\sup_a\mathbb E\sum_t r_t(a)-\sup_a\mathbb E\sum_t r_t^H(a).
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1555:./logs/iter_002_solver.stderr.log:698:Goal: Formalize examples where \(\beta\) fails but average truncation succeeds, and where both \(\beta\) and \(\Delta\) fail but regret is still zero due to known structure.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1556:./logs/iter_002_solver.stderr.log:700:Candidates: Rare bad histories for essential-sup failure; tiny-price long-delay blocks for value-free delay; terminal-spike singleton for predictable delay.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1565:./logs/iter_002_solver.stderr.log:751:- The essential-sup tail condition \(\beta(h)\to0\) is accepted as a sufficient condition under exogenous/oblivious prices, via truncation, grid discretization, and delayed full-information experts.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1567:./logs/iter_002_solver.stderr.log:756:- Formalize the upper theorem with \(\delta_T(H)\), \(\sup_a\), horizon-indexed classes, and a cited/proved delayed-experts lemma.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1568:./logs/iter_002_solver.stderr.log:761:- The original “\(\beta(h)\to0\) iff sublinear regret” conjecture is refuted under law-aware fixed-process quantifiers by iid uniform prices.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1569:./logs/iter_002_solver.stderr.log:762:- \(\Delta_T(H)=o(T)\) is not necessary: the known terminal-spike singleton has zero regret but linear truncation loss.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1635:./logs/iter_002_solver.stderr.log:1276:The original “sublinear regret iff \(\beta(h)\to0\)” is false. Under a law-aware exogenous model, no tail condition is needed at all. Under a uniform/minimax-over-unknown-class model, the clean upper bound is known, and the hidden-terminal construction gives a real lower-bound mechanism, but I do not yet have a full necessary-and-sufficient characterization.
