./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2293:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:619:Why it matters: If the law is known, playing a maximizer of \(G_T(a)=\sum_{L=0}^{T-1}a(1-a^L)\) every round matches the fixed comparator in expectation, giving zero regret despite failed \(\beta\).
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2296:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:625:Facts: For each early quote, expected reward is \(a(1-a)\), maximized at \(a=1/2\). The learner can quote \(1/2\) and get zero regret. For any \(H=o(T)\), most early rewards are excluded by truncation, so \(\Delta_T(H)\approx (T-H)/4=\Omega(T)\).
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2301:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:641:## Rare bad histories can break essential sup without large average loss
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2302:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:643:Facts needed: Essential supremum over histories can keep \(\beta(H)\) large, while \(\Delta_T(H)/T\) remains small because the histories almost never occur.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2303:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:644:Why it matters: Would show strict weakness of average truncation criteria compared with \(\beta\).
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2304:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:648:## Tiny-price long-delay blocks may break raw beta without meaningful regret
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2305:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:650:Facts needed: For every \(h\), some block creates a conditional state with \(\Pr(h<\tau<\infty)=1\), so \(\beta(h)=1\). But if \(p_m\) decreases fast enough, total best fixed comparator value may be \(o(T)\), making sublinear regret trivial.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2307:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:666:Attempt: Prove that sublinear regret is possible only if the essential-sup hitting tail \(\beta(h)\to0\).
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2308:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:667:Failure: False under law-aware/process-wise quantifiers. For iid uniform prices, \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\), but a learner knowing the law can play the best fixed quote in expectation and get zero regret.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2309:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:673:Failure: Singleton terminal-spike law has zero law-aware regret but \(\Delta_T(H)=\Omega(T)\) for \(H=o(T)\).
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2312:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:691:Failure: For fixed \(D\), delay is bounded by \(D\), so asymptotic regret is \(O(D)=o(T)\) and \(\beta(h)=0\) for \(h\ge D\).
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2316:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:707:\delta_T^P(H)=\sup_a\mathbb E\sum_t r_t(a)-\sup_a\mathbb E\sum_t r_t^H(a).
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2323:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:724:Goal: Formalize examples where \(\beta\) fails but average truncation succeeds, and where both \(\beta\) and \(\Delta\) fail but regret is still zero due to known structure.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2324:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:726:Candidates: Rare bad histories for essential-sup failure; tiny-price long-delay blocks for value-free delay; terminal-spike singleton for predictable delay.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2329:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:755:{"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"Cannot get sharp necessity; upper theorem may just restate existing sufficient route if delayed-experts citation remains external.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","rate optimization"],"summary":"Prove and sharpen the exogenous-process theorem using the comparator-gap truncation loss delta_T(H), including exact horizon-indexed quantifiers, sup over quotes, grid rounding, and a self-contained delayed-experts bound or precise citation. Ask whether rates can be expressed as inf_{H,K}{sqrt(T H log K)+T/K+Delta(H)} or with delta, and optimize for polynomial/log tails.","title":"Finalize comparator-gap upper theorem","why_promising":"This is the most solid accepted path and gives a theorem that is true even after beta-necessity and Delta-necessity failed."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"May only yield finite-horizon or horizon-dependent class lower bounds, not a fixed-process characterization.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","lower bound"],"summary":"Formalize the hidden-terminal two-world lower bound and generalize it into a reusable lemma: if two laws have identical observable prefixes for D rounds but their expected delayed reward curves have Bayes gap gamma per early round, then any uniform learner over the class has regret at least gamma D in one world. Explore q-scaled reveal probability and multiple separated intervals.","title":"General hidden-terminal lower-bound lemma","why_promising":"It isolates the real obstruction as valuable, indistinguishable delayed reward rather than raw delay probability."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The proposed condition may become a lower-bound template rather than a necessary-and-sufficient characterization; predictability is hard to encode cleanly.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","counterexample search"],"summary":"Develop a refined minimax characterization in terms of predictable versus unpredictable truncation loss. For a class C_T, define an information-theoretic tail obstruction as the maximal Bayes gap caused by rewards arriving after H among laws indistinguishable up to H, and compare it to the sufficient delta_T(H). Seek a theorem of the form sublinear regret iff there are H_T=o(T/log T) for which both short-delay learning cost and this indistinguishable-tail modulus are o(T).","title":"Information modulus for delayed value","why_promising":"Known singleton counterexamples show raw beta and Delta are not necessary because the learner can know or predict the delayed reward curve; this direction adds the missing ignorance component."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Constructions may be technically messy around strict inequality P>a, quote a=0, and all-horizon asymptotics.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","toy model"],"summary":"Produce explicit strict-separation examples: (a) rare bad histories make essential-sup beta fail while average delta or Delta is o(T); (b) deterministic long zero blocks followed by prices p_m decreasing fast enough give beta(h)=1 along arbitrarily large h but total comparator value o(T); (c) terminal-spike singleton keeps Delta_T(H)=Omega(T) but law-aware regret zero. Compute delta, Delta, beta, and optimal value for each.","title":"Strict counterexample suite","why_promising":"These examples will pin down exactly which necessity claims are false and force any final characterization to include value and information."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"A single fixed process may still wash out per-block gaps under expectation against one fixed comparator, as the iid block-splicing attempt did.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","lower bound"],"summary":"Revisit asymptotic fixed-process lower bounds using deterministic super-growing blocks rather than iid blocks. Make horizons T_n end just before a block's terminal reveal, with block n dominating all previous blocks, and choose block type or terminal distribution so the best fixed quote for horizon T_n differs from what is learnable before reveal. Check carefully that regret is against sup_a E sum_t r_t(a), not E sup_a.","title":"Super-growing block obstruction","why_promising":"If it works, it would bridge the gap between finite-horizon minimax lower bounds and a genuine fixed-law or horizon-wise asymptotic obstruction."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"May conclude that no meaningful theorem is possible without stronger protocol restrictions; action-dependent counterfactuals can be underdefined.","suggested_lenses":["model audit","formalization"],"summary":"Audit the model variants: exogenous stochastic process, oblivious adversarial sequence, adaptive non-anticipating but action-independent process, and action-dependent market response. For each, define what comparator rewards mean and whether the truncated reward vector is fixed enough for full-information delayed experts. Try to construct a simple action-dependent example where the current upper proof invalidly uses unplayed counterfactual rewards.","title":"Quantifier and causality audit","why_promising":"The upper bound and any necessity statement depend on this modeling choice; resolving it prevents proving theorems for the wrong protocol."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Meta-learning over H may introduce extra delays and lose rates; lower bounds may show adaptation costs are unavoidable.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","rate optimization"],"summary":"Design a horizon-aware but tail-profile-free algorithm that competes over a grid of truncation horizons H and quote grids K, using doubling or a master over delayed learners. Target regret close to inf_H,K [sqrt(T H log K)+T/K+delta_T(H)] plus polylog overhead, under exogenous prices.","title":"Adaptive truncation without tail knowledge","why_promising":"A characterization is more useful if the algorithm does not need to know beta, Delta, or delta in advance; it also tests whether the upper-bound condition is operational."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2330:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:766:- The essential-sup tail condition \(\beta(h)\to0\) is accepted as a sufficient condition under exogenous/oblivious prices, via truncation, grid discretization, and delayed full-information experts.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2332:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:771:- Formalize the upper theorem with \(\delta_T(H)\), \(\sup_a\), horizon-indexed classes, and a cited/proved delayed-experts lemma.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2333:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:776:- The original “\(\beta(h)\to0\) iff sublinear regret” conjecture is refuted under law-aware fixed-process quantifiers by iid uniform prices.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2334:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:777:- \(\Delta_T(H)=o(T)\) is not necessary: the known terminal-spike singleton has zero regret but linear truncation loss.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2336:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:806:The original “sublinear regret iff \(\beta(h)\to0\)” is false. Under a law-aware exogenous model, no tail condition is needed at all. Under a uniform/minimax-over-unknown-class model, the clean upper bound is known, and the hidden-terminal construction gives a real lower-bound mechanism, but I do not yet have a full necessary-and-sufficient characterization.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2346:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:847:\sqrt{T H\log K}+T/K+\sup_{P\in\mathcal C_T}\delta_T^P(H),
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2350:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:864:\sup_{P\in\mathcal C_T}\delta_T^P(H_T)=o(T).
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2352:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:893:This is sharper than using \(\Delta_T(H)=\sup_a\sum_t\mathbb E[r_t(a)-r_t^H(a)]\).
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2360:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:931:- IID uniform prices have \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\), but a law-aware learner has \(O(1)\) regret, in fact the oracle lemma covers this and more.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2361:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:933:- The terminal-spike singleton has linear \(\Delta_T(H)\) for \(H=o(T)\), but known-law regret is zero.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2362:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:935:- Rare bad histories can make essential-sup \(\beta\) large while average truncation loss is negligible.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2372:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:977:{"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The lemma may be only an oracle result with no computational content, and it fails if prices are action-dependent or if the learner is not allowed to know the law.","suggested_lenses":["proof verification","model audit"],"summary":"Stress-test the solver's law-aware oracle lemma: for any known exogenous law, conditional maximization over a K-grid gives regret at most T/K against the best fixed quote. Check measurability, strict threshold P>a, nonattainment of sup_a, randomized learners, horizon-dependent laws, and whether the proof survives adaptive but action-independent price processes.","title":"Verify known-law vacuity","why_promising":"If correct, it decisively rules out any nontrivial fixed-law tail necessity theorem and forces the final characterization to be minimax or ignorance-based."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"This may remain only sufficient; the delayed-experts theorem still needs an exact statement or proof.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","rate optimization"],"summary":"Turn the comparator-gap truncation result into a polished theorem for unknown exogenous classes: regret bounded by sqrt(T H log K)+T/K+sup_P delta_T^P(H). Include exact quantifiers for horizon-indexed classes, one uniform algorithm, grid discretization, and delayed full-information feedback.","title":"Finalize delta upper theorem","why_promising":"This is the strongest accepted upper-bound route and subsumes the older beta-based sufficient condition."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The condition may become a family of lower-bound templates rather than a clean iff characterization.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","lower bound"],"summary":"Define an information-theoretic 'valuable unpredictable tail' modulus. Start from two laws that are observationally identical for D rounds but have delayed reward curves g0,g1 with Bayes gap Gamma = avg_i sup_a gi(a) - sup_a avg_i gi(a). Explore whether minimax regret is characterized by the largest such hidden tail gap across scales.","title":"Hidden-value information modulus","why_promising":"Existing counterexamples show raw delay and truncation loss are not necessary; the missing ingredient is unpredictability of valuable delayed rewards."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"It may only yield finite-horizon or horizon-dependent class lower bounds, not fixed-process asymptotics.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","counterexample search"],"summary":"Generalize the hidden-terminal two-world construction. Prove a clean lemma with D identical prefix rounds and terminal distributions chosen to create separated optimal quotes. Then build q-scaled variants where reveal probability q makes both tail mass and regret scale like qD, and compare to upper terms T beta(H) or delta_T(H).","title":"q-scaled hidden-terminal lower bounds","why_promising":"This could produce matching lower bounds for classes with controlled delayed valuable mass, not merely a qualitative obstruction."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"Earlier iid block-splicing failed because the comparator is sup_a E sum_t r_t(a); super-growing blocks may still wash out under expectation.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","asymptotic lower bound"],"summary":"Investigate deterministic or random super-growing block constructions for a fixed infinite process. Make selected horizons dominated by one unresolved block before its terminal reveal, while earlier blocks are negligible, and check whether a fixed expected comparator can still force linear or near-linear regret.","title":"Fixed-process super-growing blocks","why_promising":"If successful, this bridges the gap between finite-horizon minimax lower bounds and an asymptotic fixed-law obstruction."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Examples may be technically easy but not lead directly to a theorem.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","sanity checks"],"summary":"Formalize strict-separation examples: rare histories that keep essential-sup beta large while average delta is small; tiny-price long-delay blocks where beta fails but total comparator value is o(T); and known terminal-spike laws where Delta_T(H) is linear but law-aware regret is zero. Compute beta, Delta, delta, and comparator value explicitly.","title":"Counterexample suite for false necessity","why_promising":"These examples will prevent explorers from proving false tail characterizations and clarify which objects are merely sufficient."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The current full-information reduction may be invalid or underdefined in action-dependent markets.","suggested_lenses":["model audit","counterexample search"],"summary":"Audit exogenous, oblivious adversarial, adaptive action-independent, and action-dependent price models. For each, define the comparator path and counterfactual rewards for unplayed quotes. Try to build a minimal action-dependent example where truncated reward vectors are not fixed by the realized path, breaking the delayed-experts proof.","title":"Causality and comparator conventions","why_promising":"The correct theorem depends on this modeling choice; without it the upper bound and lower-bound quantifiers may be proving statements for different games."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
