./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:977:{"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The lemma may be only an oracle result with no computational content, and it fails if prices are action-dependent or if the learner is not allowed to know the law.","suggested_lenses":["proof verification","model audit"],"summary":"Stress-test the solver's law-aware oracle lemma: for any known exogenous law, conditional maximization over a K-grid gives regret at most T/K against the best fixed quote. Check measurability, strict threshold P>a, nonattainment of sup_a, randomized learners, horizon-dependent laws, and whether the proof survives adaptive but action-independent price processes.","title":"Verify known-law vacuity","why_promising":"If correct, it decisively rules out any nontrivial fixed-law tail necessity theorem and forces the final characterization to be minimax or ignorance-based."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"This may remain only sufficient; the delayed-experts theorem still needs an exact statement or proof.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","rate optimization"],"summary":"Turn the comparator-gap truncation result into a polished theorem for unknown exogenous classes: regret bounded by sqrt(T H log K)+T/K+sup_P delta_T^P(H). Include exact quantifiers for horizon-indexed classes, one uniform algorithm, grid discretization, and delayed full-information feedback.","title":"Finalize delta upper theorem","why_promising":"This is the strongest accepted upper-bound route and subsumes the older beta-based sufficient condition."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The condition may become a family of lower-bound templates rather than a clean iff characterization.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","lower bound"],"summary":"Define an information-theoretic 'valuable unpredictable tail' modulus. Start from two laws that are observationally identical for D rounds but have delayed reward curves g0,g1 with Bayes gap Gamma = avg_i sup_a gi(a) - sup_a avg_i gi(a). Explore whether minimax regret is characterized by the largest such hidden tail gap across scales.","title":"Hidden-value information modulus","why_promising":"Existing counterexamples show raw delay and truncation loss are not necessary; the missing ingredient is unpredictability of valuable delayed rewards."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"It may only yield finite-horizon or horizon-dependent class lower bounds, not fixed-process asymptotics.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","counterexample search"],"summary":"Generalize the hidden-terminal two-world construction. Prove a clean lemma with D identical prefix rounds and terminal distributions chosen to create separated optimal quotes. Then build q-scaled variants where reveal probability q makes both tail mass and regret scale like qD, and compare to upper terms T beta(H) or delta_T(H).","title":"q-scaled hidden-terminal lower bounds","why_promising":"This could produce matching lower bounds for classes with controlled delayed valuable mass, not merely a qualitative obstruction."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"Earlier iid block-splicing failed because the comparator is sup_a E sum_t r_t(a); super-growing blocks may still wash out under expectation.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","asymptotic lower bound"],"summary":"Investigate deterministic or random super-growing block constructions for a fixed infinite process. Make selected horizons dominated by one unresolved block before its terminal reveal, while earlier blocks are negligible, and check whether a fixed expected comparator can still force linear or near-linear regret.","title":"Fixed-process super-growing blocks","why_promising":"If successful, this bridges the gap between finite-horizon minimax lower bounds and an asymptotic fixed-law obstruction."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Examples may be technically easy but not lead directly to a theorem.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","sanity checks"],"summary":"Formalize strict-separation examples: rare histories that keep essential-sup beta large while average delta is small; tiny-price long-delay blocks where beta fails but total comparator value is o(T); and known terminal-spike laws where Delta_T(H) is linear but law-aware regret is zero. Compute beta, Delta, delta, and comparator value explicitly.","title":"Counterexample suite for false necessity","why_promising":"These examples will prevent explorers from proving false tail characterizations and clarify which objects are merely sufficient."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The current full-information reduction may be invalid or underdefined in action-dependent markets.","suggested_lenses":["model audit","counterexample search"],"summary":"Audit exogenous, oblivious adversarial, adaptive action-independent, and action-dependent price models. For each, define the comparator path and counterfactual rewards for unplayed quotes. Try to build a minimal action-dependent example where truncated reward vectors are not fixed by the realized path, breaking the delayed-experts proof.","title":"Causality and comparator conventions","why_promising":"The correct theorem depends on this modeling choice; without it the upper bound and lower-bound quantifiers may be proving statements for different games."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
