./logs/iter_003_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stdout.log:13:- **Plausible but incomplete:** The envelope claim “if \(q\le B(D)\), then \(\beta(h)\le B(h)\)” assumes \(B\) is nonincreasing and that the relevant tail is the finite-horizon/absorbing extension tail. Without monotonicity, the right condition is \(q\le \inf_{h<D} B(h)\).
./logs/iter_003_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stdout.log:32:- **Post-horizon stress test:** If after \(T=D+1\) the process later emits large prices, the claimed \(\beta(h)=0\) for \(h\ge D\) fails, while the regret lower bound over horizon \(T\) is unchanged. So raw \(\beta\) is not pinned down by the finite-horizon construction unless the extension is fixed.
