./logs/iter_003_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:722:## iid uniform prices refute raw beta necessity
./logs/iter_003_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:728:Thus \(\beta(h)=\sup_{a<1}a^h=1\) for every \(h\).
./logs/iter_003_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:729:Why it matters: If the law is known, playing a maximizer of \(G_T(a)=\sum_{L=0}^{T-1}a(1-a^L)\) every round matches the fixed comparator in expectation, giving zero regret despite failed \(\beta\).
./logs/iter_003_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:753:Facts: For each early quote, expected reward is \(a(1-a)\), maximized at \(a=1/2\). The learner can quote \(1/2\) and get zero regret. For any \(H=o(T)\), most early rewards are excluded by truncation, so \(\Delta_T(H)\approx (T-H)/4=\Omega(T)\).
./logs/iter_003_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:769:## Rare bad histories can break essential sup without large average loss
./logs/iter_003_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:771:Facts needed: Essential supremum over histories can keep \(\beta(H)\) large, while \(\Delta_T(H)/T\) remains small because the histories almost never occur.
./logs/iter_003_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:772:Why it matters: Would show strict weakness of average truncation criteria compared with \(\beta\).
./logs/iter_003_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:776:## Tiny-price long-delay blocks may break raw beta without meaningful regret
./logs/iter_003_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:778:Facts needed: For every \(h\), some block creates a conditional state with \(\Pr(h<\tau<\infty)=1\), so \(\beta(h)=1\). But if \(p_m\) decreases fast enough, total best fixed comparator value may be \(o(T)\), making sublinear regret trivial.
