./logs/iter_002_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:769:Thus \(\beta(h)=\sup_{a<1}a^h=1\) for every \(h\).
./logs/iter_002_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:770:Why it matters: If the law is known, playing a maximizer of \(G_T(a)=\sum_{L=0}^{T-1}a(1-a^L)\) every round matches the fixed comparator in expectation, giving zero regret despite failed \(\beta\).
./logs/iter_002_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:776:Facts: For each early quote, expected reward is \(a(1-a)\), maximized at \(a=1/2\). The learner can quote \(1/2\) and get zero regret. For any \(H=o(T)\), most early rewards are excluded by truncation, so \(\Delta_T(H)\approx (T-H)/4=\Omega(T)\).
./logs/iter_002_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:792:## Rare bad histories can break essential sup without large average loss
